<p class=MsoNormal align="center"><img border="0" src="ensemble.jpg" width="250" height="188"></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><font size="2" face="Verdana">On Friday July 17th, I had the
chance to visit Ensemble Studios in Dallas, Tx. The offices were grand and the
atmosphere was festive. I had a chance to take a look at Age of Empires: Age of
Kings which was in a word, impressive. After the tournament, I had a chance to
speak with Sandy Petersen, a veteran of the gaming industry and as I would soon
find out, someone who really enjoys what he does.</font></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><font face="Verdana" size="2"><i><b>(Please note that this
was transcribed from tape)</b></i></font></p>

<table>
  <tr>
    <td width="382" bgcolor="#003366">
      <p align="center">&nbsp;<b><font color="white" size="2" face="verdana,arial,helvetica">The
      Interview</font></b></td>
</table>

<p class=MsoNormal><b><span style='font-size:10.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:Verdana'><font face="Verdana" size="2">[Wewp] How long has this been in development for you guys
(Ensemble)?</font></span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:10.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:Verdana'><font face="Verdana" size="2">Well, I guess we started the very early parts of it right
after we shipped Age of Empires, but we really didn’t get underway until the
following spring around 98.</font></span></p>

<h1><span style='font-size:10.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;font-family:Verdana'><font face="Verdana" size="2">[Wewp]
Yea,
because I remember seeing a little snippet of it at E3 in ‘98.</font></span></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:10.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:Verdana'><font face="Verdana" size="2">Yea we had the same basic engine with updated graphics
etc.</font></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><b><i><span style='font-size:10.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:Verdana'><font face="Verdana" size="2">At this point there was a problem with his machine</font></span></i></b></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText><span style='font-size:10.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:Verdana'><b><font face="Verdana" size="2">[Wewp] I’ve seen that before, (laugh) I guess this is a good time
to ask is Age of Empires II gonna ship on Linux?</font></b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:10.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:Verdana'><font face="Verdana" size="2">&nbsp;(Laugh) Ok,<span style="mso-spacerun: yes">  </span>I’m a
designer not a programmer or expert in systems..</font></span></p>

<h1><span style='font-size:10.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:Verdana'><b><font face="Verdana" size="2">[Wewp] </font></b><font face="Verdana" size="2">So
you’re not in charge of porting.</font></span></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style="font-size: 10.0pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: Verdana"><font face="Verdana" size="2">(Laugh)
I’m one of the least technical guys here, when something’s
wrong with my system, I’ll usually go next door to the programmers and say
&quot;Help, It's locked up, there’s a blue screen,” you know, so I know just
enough to be dangerous…to myself (Smiling)… But the scripting system we’re
using now to do the AI.</font></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style="font-size: 10.0pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: Verdana"><font face="Verdana" size="2">(Describing his role) – see the programmer designs the
scripts and I put them together, I guess that's kind of like programming, but it's
not really my primary life function to program.</font></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:10.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:Verdana'><font face="Verdana" size="2">We’ve been working on the game for about 2 to 2 ½ years,
it’s using pre-existing technology, all the art is new and a lot of the
fundamental things in the game have changed. It's not just the same thing with
new stuff, for instance the artificial intelligence is totally different from
the ground up, there’s no resemblance at all. In the new one (Age of Empires
II) there are these scripts that go through using the expert system where it
picks out what it's going to do and it has these rules that evaluate any
given moment. Before it was very reactive and simple, it just had a build list
and it tried to go through the build list in order, and now it doesn’t do that,
it’ll look at the build list and say, well I don’t wanna build this yet because
I don’t have a market yet or whatever it’s trying to do, so there’s a lot going
in the AI. Basically it’s acting like a human. And of course we’re trying to
get better multiplayer stuff too. Multiplayer has always been something we like
the most too.</font></span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText><span style='font-size:10.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:Verdana'><b><font face="Verdana" size="2">[Wewp] </font></b><font face="Verdana" size="2"><b>One thing that I’ve really noticed is that it seems like
it's on a more grand scale as far as the single player aspect or the campaign
that you guys have.</b></font></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:10.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:Verdana'><font face="Verdana" size="2">Yes, we started some of that trend with Rise of Rome where
we had actual historical based campaigns and this (AoE II) continues that but
with more fancied up touches with the music and the voiceovers. So you get kind
of the progression through time, so when you're being Joan of Arc, Genghis Khan
or whatever you’re actually able to see the stuff happen and react. I think a
big improvement is that it draws you out into the game instead of just trying
to finish the campaign it makes you think like “I wanna see what happens to
Joan,” or whoever it is, so I think that campaigns are head and shoulders above
Age I.</font></span></p>

<h1><span style='font-size:10.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;font-family:Verdana'><font face="Verdana" size="2">[Wewp]
It’s
really good because it educates us as well as entertains.</font></span></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style="font-size: 10.0pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: Verdana"><font face="Verdana" size="2">Yeah and we try to be fairly accurate historically on the
campaigns. Whenever there’s a question of whether it should be fun or accurate,
we always go on the side of fun, because we are a game company. Like for
example, in the Siege of Aker, two of the trebuchet that the crusaders have are
named “God’s Own Sling “ and “Bad Neighbor” and they actually were named that
in the real battle, so it's kind of cool to have that sort of thing.</font></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:10.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:Verdana'><font face="Verdana" size="2"><i><b>(At this point the game starts up and we head to the
campaign section)</b></i></font></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:10.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:Verdana'><font face="Verdana" size="2">Well this is the William Wallace learning campaign.</font></span></p>

<h1><span style='font-size:10.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;font-family:Verdana'><font face="Verdana" size="2">[Wewp]
So
you actually go through and it teaches you how to play the game?</font></span></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:10.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:Verdana'><font face="Verdana" size="2">Yea, it starts out very simple and unlike
before with campaigns,
you don't have to go through them all if you don’t want to. In this campaign the first battle is extremely simple.</font></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><b><span style='font-size:10.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:Verdana'><font face="Verdana" size="2">[Wewp] (Smiling) OK…</font></span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:10.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:Verdana'><font face="Verdana" size="2">The idea is that you’re someone’s grandmother, you’ve
never played a role-playing or RTS game before in your life and you’re sitting
down trying to figure it out.</font></span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><b><span style='font-size:10.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:Verdana'><font face="Verdana" size="2"><i>The intro begins, which is very
grand. We listen to
the voiceovers in the game and discuss the in house studio where they recorded.
Sandy begins to walk through the tutorial in which you point and click to areas
chosen, in essence learning to march. Then the tutorial leads the character on
screen through the “scary” black area, teaching potential gamers about the fog
of war familiar in Real Time Strategy games.</i></font></span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:10.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:Verdana'><font face="Verdana" size="2">If you’ve never played an RTS game before you’d never know
about things like this. Microsoft had groups of newbies that didn’t know
anything about computers. They sat them down and said “Ok, here ya go,” and they didn’t know
anything, they didn’t know they could click on a guy and make him go somewhere,
you know things we take for granted when we play a game.</font></span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText><span style='font-size:10.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:Verdana'><font face="Verdana" size="2"><b>Things like that help you think a little differently and
in the end you make a better game as well.</b></font></span></p>

<font face="Verdana" size="2">

<span style="font-size: 10.0pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt; font-family: Verdana; mso-fareast-font-family: Times New Roman; mso-bidi-font-family: Times New Roman; mso-ansi-language: EN-US; mso-fareast-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-language: HE">Yeah,
because none of this is going to hurt an advanced player that’s playing because
they’ll figure out pretty fast that “Oh, I’m learning how to walk through fog,
I should skip this..” and you can just skip it, but a beginner doesn’t know it.
The key we’ve always had in our games is that we’ve tried to have enough depth
for the hardcore guy, with the strategy and all the various options and yet
easy enough for someone to tell what’s going on for a newbie, you know? Part of
it is by making it historical so if you are moving (your guys) and you’re
fairly new to gaming, you can still know that a knight in armor on horseback is
going to be faster that a foot soldier and you can figure out what the units
are, whereas playing say, fantasy or science fiction it's not real clear to you
whether this starship is better than that starship or this alien is better than
that alien. So that helps us a little bit by making it historical.</span></font>
<p align="center"><font size="2" face="Verdana"><a href="Sandy2.asp">Page 2</font></a></p>
